speechtext:
Because I have seen the official figures. The Prime Minister went on:
In British Columbia at the present time the unemployed number 7,692, and it is expected that during the winter that number will increase to 14,700.
At the present time, Mr. Speaker, according to a recent statement made by the Minister of Labour of the province of British Columbia, there are over twenty thousand unemployed' in my province. That shows the success of the policies of the right hon. gentleman. The Prime Minister then goes on to give the total figures:
It will be observed that the present number is something over 117,000, and it is believed "at that number will approximate 177,000 dur-mg the winter. If the measures which we submit to this house are productive of the good which we anticipate, we hope that that uumber will steadily decrease, rather than increase, and that the maximum anticipated number of 1/7,000 will not be reached. . . . The figure ot 200,000 would seem to me to be almost incredible in this country at this time.
But, Mr. Speaker, the fact is that if you make a careful examination of the unemployment situation throughout Canada to-day, you will find that there are over 300,000 unemployed throughout the Dominion. So I am .forced to this conclusion, that the government has failed absolutely and completely not only bo end unemployment, but even to remedy it, as promised by the Prime Minister during the election campaign; and secondly, that the policies which the government adopted in the special session for that purpose have been proven a gigantic and grotesque failure.
I wish now to approach a phase of the situation which possibly may not disturb so seriously the tender susceptibilities of hon. gentlemen opposite. I wish to review the present situation in the light of world conditions, because despite the accusations unfairly and unwarrantably made against the late administration by the right hon. the Prime Minister and his colleagues, blaming the late government for the conditions of depression which we have had during the last twelve months, I for one believe that the present situation has been mainly, if not practically entirely, caused by world-wide conditions. But, sir, the situation in the Dominion of Canada has been intensified and accentuated by the hopeless tariff policies initiated by this government last September, which have put ramparts of ruin around the Dominion and exhausted the consuming and purchasing power of the ordinary Canadian worker. I desire now to place upon Hansard, 
without comment, the causes of the worldwide depression, as I have culled them from some of the leading economists of the world:
1.	Stock gambling and deflation of unwarranted stock values, and withdrawal of credit improperly extended;
2.	Shortage of purchasing power due to defective distribution of credit;
3.	Under consumption due to lack of confidence or loss of purchasing power;
4.	Overproduction in natural resources products, manufactured commodities, buildings and industrial equipment;
5.	Tariff walls prohibitory to trade;
6.	Modern efficiency in business and industry increasing production and distribution per man;
7.	Failure of the established foundation of credit, due to
(a)	Return to the gold standard
(b)	Shortage of gold
(c)	Mal-distribution of gold
(d)	Failure to accept silver as a support for gold in foundationing credit and currency.
8.	National debts;
9.	Inevitable decline of confidence as a reaction from the previous undue optimism;
16. Rise and aggressiveness of Russia.
I should like to summarize those ten causes under three heads. The real cause of the depression to-day-this is not my own opinion, but one I have gathered from the various authorities which I have consulted-has been, first, the appalling national obligations now extant; secondly, the impossibility of distributing the limited supply of gold to meet the credit demands that the liquidation period has developed; and thirdly, the rise of Russia as a force and power in international affairs and world trade.
